Dear Colleagues,
As we reflect on the legacy we are building, we remain deeply 
committed to our society and to the role we play in its future. We 
will continue to support Croatia’s development and work toward 
making it the most AI-empowered society in Europe. This ambition 
is bold, but I am convinced that no company is better positioned 
than ours to make it real for every individual and every enterprise 
across the country. Technology enables this journey, yet people are 
its true driving force.
Our purpose is clear: to empower everyone in Croatia to shape their 
future. At its heart lies a simple belief, “I can.” For our employees, 
this means taking ownership of our growth, supporting one 
another, and striving to reach our full potential. For our customers, 
it means enabling them to explore new technologies, adapt with 
confidence, and progress at their own pace. For society, it means 
inspiring every person to feel capable of taking a meaningful step 
forward.
AI can transform how we work, learn, teach, heal, and compete. 
The opportunities are vast, and the responsibility is ours.
As Hrvatski Telekom, we carry Croatia in our name. With pride and 
responsibility, we can once again prove that we are the strongest 
partner and most reliable support for everyone to say, “I can.” 
Because we are Hrvatski Telekom. And if anyone can and should 
make this happen, it is us.
Through our Big Picture conversations, I invite you to align on 
our shared direction and to define clear personal and team action 
points that will turn our ambition into reality. I look forward to 
what we will achieve together.
Nataša

4
1: Visual Dialogue
The picture is tested with about 10% of 
employees and so provides valuable insights and 
feedback to the leadership team in the form of 
qualitative data. The objective of the process is 
to engage the entire organisation and provide 
an opportunity for people to make sense of 
the change ahead, what it means for them and 
encourage them to take ownership of what they 
could do to help achieve the desired vision.
We use the William Bridges model of change as a 
framework within which to structure the data we 
receive during the process. 
This looks at “where we’ve come from”, “where are 
we now”, “where do we want to get to” and how do 
we get there”
Visual Dialogue is a change process 
that uses pictures to get people 
talking about complex and/or 
difficult issues, such as a new 
strategy or change programme. 
The process we take to create the 
Big Picture is iterative and involves 
input from both leadership and 
employees ensuring it is co-created. 
The Big Picture is designed to be 
a tool for conversation and by cocreating it we endeavour to make 
sure it reflects the history, current 
challenges and vision in a way that 
everyone can recognise and make 
sense of. 

5
The Big Picture, we believe, is most effective when used in Visual Dialogue 
sessions, that are intended to do the following three things:
• Convey important information about where a business is going and why.
• Help colleagues to make sense of the change and what it means to them 
personally through discussion with their peers.
• Gather useful insights and ideas to support the change process.
In a Visual Dialogue session, a facilitator uses a Big Picture to introduce people 
to the change journey the organisation is on. The facilitator shares their own 
story about where the business has come from, where it’s going and how it’s 
going to get there. Once they have shared the story, the facilitator then helps 
the group to discuss the issues raised.
“Dialogue is the only important human skill that 
we don’t train people how to do effectively” 
Peter Nixon

6
1: Visual Dialogue
Big Picture facilitation role
The Big Picture is a powerful 
catalyst for conversation and you 
will find that most people will want 
to talk about what they see in it. In 
a story sharing session, people learn 
more from each other than from 
the picture itself or the facilitator 
running the session. Your role will be 
to set up and manage the discussion 
to encourage that learning. 
To do this, you will need to 
prepare yourself, your materials 
and the space properly. You’ll 
need to collaborate with the key 
stakeholders in your area to agree 
the outcomes they need from these 
discussions. 
You’ll also need to manage the 
expectations of the people 
attending the session so they 
understand why they’re there and 
what’s expected of them.
The Visual Dialogue process and the 
feedback it generates can act as a 
bridge between the workforce and 
the senior leadership team, helping 
each other to understand concerns 
better. Your job is to help build that 
bridge.
Key benefits:
• More engagement; gets people 
talking
• Generates peer learning and 
sharing
• Shifts understanding
• Creates a common language 
across the business
• Gives people a ‘big picture’ 
view

7
Personal development and 
support
Facilitating a Big Picture session 
can be a challenge even for an 
accomplished presenter because it 
requires you to put aside your own 
views and concentrate on enabling 
a group of colleagues to explore 
and share theirs. To do this also 
requires a high level of awareness 
of what’s going on above the level 
of the detail that’s being talked 
about: where is the conversation 
going? Who is speaking too much? 
Who isn’t speaking at all? How 
are people feeling? Where could 
the conversation go now? What, 
if anything, do I need to do at this 
point? 
The skills that facilitating a Big 
Picture discussion develops are 
transferable and can enhance your 
personal effectiveness at work, 
regardless of your role. It can be 
quite demanding and takes a lot of 
energy to deliver it well. With this 
in mind, we strongly recommend 
that you don’t schedule more than 
two 90 minute sessions in any one 
day; regardless of the pressures on 
time and resources. This will ensure 
that you, the participants and the 
business get the best possible 
benefit from the activity.
We would also encourage you to 
connect with your colleagues who 
have also been running sessions 
to share experiences about what 
worked or didn’t work and discuss 
what came up.
“Real dialogues happen when people listen 
to each other and learn from each other”
R. Wegerif

8
2: The Session
Running a Story Sharer session: a 
step-by-step guide
1. Welcome, introductions
2. Positioning
3. Setting expectations for the 
session
4. Purpose and outcomes
5. Close up look at the Big Picture
6. The Story
7. The Discussion
8. Close
Some general guidelines for running 
a session
• The planned group size 
should be between 8-12 
people. With natural fall out 
this will mean average group 
sizes of 7-10 people.
• The sessions should be 
planned to be diverse, mixed 
groups of individuals where 
possible.

9
Step 1. Welcome, introductions
Welcome the group. Introduce 
yourself and invite everyone 
present to introduce themselves. If 
appropriate, get them to say where 
they’re from and something about 
their role in the company. 
Step 2. Positioning
Explain to participants how and 
why the Big Picture was created 
and about the number and range of 
people who contributed to it. Inform 
them that the picture is being used 
across the whole business to start 
conversations that help people 
make sense of the changes ahead.
Step 3. Setting expectations for the 
session
Note: as the facilitator of this 
session, you have the power to shape 
the entire session by the way you 
consciously (or unconsciously) set 
expectations for the group at the 
beginning of the session.
Be clear with your group about what 
your role will be in this session (story 
sharer / facilitator / data gatherer). 
Let them know that your job is not 
to know everything or to defend the 
vision but to get them talking about 
their views. 
If you don’t say anything, the group 
will automatically treat you as an 
‘authority’ (particularly if you’re 
senior to them) and they will default 
to a ‘Q&A’ mode. 
Step 4. Explain to your group the 
specific purpose and outcomes that 
have been agreed for the session
Tell them clearly why this session 
is taking place and what you hope 
participants will get out of it.
You might also want to set some 
behavioural expectations at this 
point. You can invite people to:

10
2: The Session
• Speak honestly and from 
personal experience
• Respect each other’s views
• Be willing to challenge any 
assumptions (including their 
own)
Step 5. Close up look at the Big 
Picture (5 mins)
Before you share the story, invite 
the group to get up close and spend 
a few minutes looking at the detail 
in the picture. The closer they get, 
the better. Stand back and let them 
explore the detail and talk among 
themselves about what they see. 
Step 6. The story (5-10 mins)
When the group has looked at the 
detail, say you will now to take them 
through the story in the picture. 
Step 7. The discussion 
Your aim is to get people talking 
about the issues in the picture, 
not about the picture itself. It is 
important to keep this distinction 
in mind to help you recognise if a 
group starts to ‘critique’ the picture. 
If this happens, remind them that 
the picture is just a start point for 
the discussion. 
You will also need to keep the 
intended focus for your session in 
mind. Intervene openly to bring 
the discussion back on track if 
necessary, referring back to the 
purpose of the session. If you have 
been clear about your role in the 
introduction to the session, your 
group will be comfortable with your 
intervention.
Step 8. Close session
It is usually quite hard to close a 
Visual Dialogue session once it has 
started. So as you reach the end 
of the time we suggest, if possible, 
that you highlight the time left, 
thank people for their participation 
and share what will happen next. 
It is useful to be provided with the 
information about next steps in 
advance.

11
Feedback
At the first stage of story sharing 
with employees, feedback is not 
always the key outcome, the 
main purpose being the sharing 
of the story of the change and 
helping employees make sense of 
it. However you may be asked to 
capture some feedback about the 
session on a form we provide. The 
purpose of this is to gain insights 
to the key themes that arose, the 
feelings of the group and your own 
personal reflections of the session.
We suggest you capture this after 
the session to avoid the session 
turning into a list-building exercise 
where people may look to you to 
action any ideas that arise in the 
discussion.
You can share this intention with 
the group at the end and that 
this will be presented back to the 
management team along with the 
feedback from the other sessions.

12
Preparing to run the session
Your role is to support the use of 
the Big Picture and to facilitate 
discussions using the Big Picture. 
Clarifying the purpose and 
outcomes of the session you are 
running and the outcomes you want 
from it, is critical. 
The simpler the purpose and 
outcomes you set, the more 
productive your sessions will be. 
The following purpose and 
outcomes sections provide a clear 
direction for the story sharing 
sessions and will support you 
to deliver a rich and valuable 
discussion. It is something you can 
return to, reminding participants of 
why they are here with you. 
Purpose
• To start a conversation that 
explores what we can do 
differently to achieve our 
vision.
• For people to understand 
the areas being focused on 
for improvement and what it 
means for them.
• To share experiences and learn 
from colleagues.
Outcomes
• To have understood the 
areas being focused on for 
improvement and what it 
means for people.
• To have explored what enables 
and blocks people from feeling 
involved in the vision.
• To have explored what, 
individually they will have to do 
differently to play a part in the 
changing culture.
• To have been listened to and to 
have listened to others.
Session preparation
When facilitating group sessions 
you will find yourself in charge 
of two kinds of space: a physical 
space and an emotional space. The 
‘physical’ space is quite familiar 
(rooms, equipment, materials, 
lighting, refreshments for example). 
The ‘emotional’ space is less familiar 
but refers to how the people in your 
group – including you – are feeling 
before, during and after the session. 
You will need to prepare and 
manage both kinds of space.
2: The Session

13
The Physical Space
Room: Choose a location large 
enough to place a semi-circle of 
chairs facing the wall where the 
picture will be positioned. Leave 
enough space between the chairs 
and the wall for the group to stand 
up close to the picture.
Lighting: Good natural lighting is 
preferable. Otherwise, ensure the 
wall is well lit with artificial lighting.
The Emotional Space
To be of service to your group you 
need to be present (i.e. free of worry 
and not ‘talking to yourself’ in your 
head). The primary ways to reduce 
your fear and worry in this process 
are to:
• Be clear on your role. You’re 
there to help other people to 
have a discussion, not to be 
right or argue a position.
• Learn the story and the detail in 
the picture and practise sharing 
it until you’re familiar with 
every part of it. 
• Take care of the logistics early 
so that you have plenty of time 
to set up without being rushed. 
• Work on your emotional 
intelligence (EQ) so you can 
learn not to fear your own or 
others’ feelings. 
• Learn to separate your own 
feelings from those of the 
people in your group (learn how 
not to be reactive).
The last two are significant life skills 
that can take time to master but 
are valuable leadership attributes. 
Facilitation is a great arena in which 
to develop these skills.

14
3: The Story
Why Stories Matter
Humans have told stories since the 
beginning of time, to teach, to learn 
and to adapt to new environments.
They’ve always been a powerful way 
to connect people through empathy 
and understanding, especially in a 
time of change or uncertainty.
Pictures are more than just a way to 
make words more memorable. 
• They articulate where words 
struggle. 
• They offer a solid structure to 
the abstraction of words. 
• They tell stories in contexts we 
can relate to. 

15
Why do organisations need 
stories?
Are they just a way to make people 
do things? To get employees to work 
harder? To control how it does things 
and is seen in the world?
Organisations, as we all do, have 
narratives:
How it started; why it exists; what its 
mission is; what its challenges were or 
are; what it wants to be in the future.
Organisational stories help to:
• Create a common purpose for 
everyone
• Create emotional connection 
and trust to that purpose.
• Agree a kind of ‘social contract’ 
on how we work together.
• To give people space to consider 
how their work supports the 
ambition of the company. 

16
3: The Story
Organisational “Internal” 
narrative
A ‘Branding’ is a narrative 
for customers, investors and 
stakeholders to state: “this is who 
we are-this is what is important 
to us”
An ‘Internal narrative’ has to be 
aligned with its brand as much 
as the brand has to reflect the 
internal narrative.
The difference is an Internal 
narrative needs to be honest 
about how things are and how 
things will be. 
I can say I am a brave warrior fighting an 
evil monster on behalf of local villagers 
but if I am actually staying in my hut, 
with no intention of going into the 
forest, of training to wield a sword or 
even owning one, then that story isn’t 
mine. People won’t believe it and won’t 
connect to it.
Someone else’s script also won’t 
be authentic for me. I need to 
make it true for me in order to 
make the case for others to follow.
Personalised Story
We suggest it is more effective 
for story sharers to personalise a 
strategic narrative in order for it to 
resonate with people.
A personalised corporate story 
includes:
• Who we are, where we came 
from
• Why we exist
• Where I came in, what’s 
important for me
• The challenges I see
• where we want to go
• How I can help to get there
• What it will be like for me
when we get there 
Benefits: A personalised narrative brings 
the story to life for people, prompting them 
to consider their story and how the story 
relates to them.
A personalised narrative allows it to be 
more passionate, humorous and speak to 
people’s lived experience of work.

17
Story Structures
Different Story structures are 
useful for different audiences.
• Elevator Pitch
A quick bullet pointed list, 30 
seconds
• High Level Story Curve
A short narrative with a key message 
as a takeaway 3 mins
• Detailed Narrative
A narrative introducing context, a 
challenge, the challenge, the change 
required and the resolution. 5-10 
mins (see Story Mountain)
Considering the audience 
It is important to adapt your story to your audience and the objective 
you want to get from the session.
If you are speaking to a wide diverse group, then consider what will 
resonate with them collectively. Keep messaging high level.
If you are speaking to a specific Team or area, then pull out points that 
will speak to their lived experience of work/life.
If the objective is to encourage people to have a discussion, then pose 
an open question to get them to interact with the story.

18
4: The Story - How to tell a good story
Rhetorical devices 
Rhetorical devices are specialised 
language techniques used to make 
writing or speech more persuasive, 
memorable, and impactful. 
These devices have been used 
since Greek and Roman times to 
connect and inspire people.
By employing patterns of sound, 
word choice, and sentence 
structure, these tools help evoke 
emotion (pathos), establish 
credibility (ethos), or appeal to 
logic (logos).
Device Examples
• Rule of 3 – “I will talk about…
Trust, Ai, Change” Central 
to Rhetoric, it makes it 
more likely that people will 
remember, believe and act on 
what you say.
• Contrast – “a focus on KPIs, a 
focus on customer experience” 
Shining a light on differences, 
contrasts, that lead to action.
• Analogy – “The national 
mindset was not like this 
when we created Croatia” - 
Analogies are used to explain 
new concepts through familiar 
ones, often acting as a bridge 
between a complex idea and a 
simple, concrete example.
• Metaphor – “Our mission is a 
long hard journey” Making a 
direct comparison by saying 
one thing is another to 
highlight a shared quality.
• Humour - Humour is a 
powerful tool that does more 
than make an people laugh; 
it acts as a “Trojan horse” to 
build emotional connections.

19
Model: Story Mountain
A classic narrative arc that builds 
engagement and meaning.
The Story Mountain Structure:
Beginning (Context)
Where are we today?
What is the context of where we 
are?
Rising Challenge
What problem, tension, or 
opportunity exists?
Why did it matter?
Peak (Turning Point)
What are we aiming to achieve
Why are we aiming for this?
Resolution
What can we do differently?
What will be the impact for us?
Ending (Future)
What does this mean for the 
future?
What’s next?

20
Guidelines for sharing the story
First of all, there is no script. We 
don’t want you to stand in front of 
a picture and read something from 
a piece of paper. We want you to 
be able to share a 5-7 minute story 
that is brought to life with examples 
from your own experience (wherever 
possible) and is relevant to the people 
you’re sharing it with. The purpose 
of this is for you to encourage the 
participants to see themselves and 
their stories through the picture.
To be able to develop your own story 
it will be useful to first read through 
the story in the back of this guide 
and try to summarise it confidently 
in one or two sentences. 
Think about how to bring the story 
to life and make it more relevant 
and meaningful for the particular 
audience you have in front of you. 
This can be achieved by using your 
own experiences and by finding 
examples that you know your 
audience will recognise and relate to. 
There is no right way or wrong way 
of telling it. The story should be 
yours and authenticity is the key, so 
don’t try to talk about something you 
have no experience of. 
There are elements in the picture 
that we have looked to include that 
are intentionally provocative, these 
are issues that have either arisen 
time and again or are things people 
have felt very passionate about. 
These are extremely likely to provoke 
a conversation.
We see the picture as having lots 
of little hooks, with key messages, 
across it for you to weave your story 
through. You may find it useful to 
plot these on the picture we have 
included at the back of this guide.
You may also feel comfortable bullet 
pointing your story to ensure you 
cover everything. That works for 
some people - however when you’re 
up in front of the picture sometimes 
the best laid plans can go out of 
the window! Don’t worry if that 
happens, there is plenty of time in 
the discussion afterwards where you 
can bring the group’s attention to 
any points you missed.
When sharing your story use the 
picture as much as possible and try 
to move people’s attention around 
it. Be aware of your positioning and 
try to engage the group through eye 
contact.
5: Guidance on the Story

21
Try to aim for between 5-7 minutes. 
It’s better to share an engaging short 
story than a long one that might be 
less so.
Keep in mind the techniques 
we have covered above when 
developing your story and consider 
where you will start, what you need 
to cover and where you will end.
If the purpose is to create a dialogue 
then aim to finish on a nice open 
question. Ask only one question and 
sit with the silence!

22
6: The Facilitation
People at this point are generally 
more concerned about the sharing 
of a story in front of a group than 
the facilitating of the discussion that 
follows. However in our experience 
the facilitative part of your role 
can also be challenging and it is 
important to prepare for it as much 
as the story.
The session can be quite counter 
to what people expect from a 
facilitated session in that the main 
outcomes are not defined objectives 
for someone to go away and 
action. Bear in mind the purpose 
of the session, that it is purely for 
people to have the first of many 
conversations about the change the 
organisation is going through. 
The main objective is for people to 
go away with a sense of what they 
could do differently to deliver the 
vision, and take ownership of the 
change required.
There are three attributes that we 
believe are essential to not only 
delivering a Visual Dialogue session 
effectively but also to any relational 
practice. These are Presence, Self 
Awareness and Curiosity.
We shall point to these throughout 
the advice that we lay out below.

23
Guidance on facilitation 
A Visual Dialogue session is likely 
to go in a different direction every 
time you run one. This is completely 
normal, the different dynamics 
of a group will alter where its 
attention goes. What is important 
is that participants get to hear a 
story about the way the business 
is changing and have a chance to 
discuss the issues that matter most 
to them. 
Opening the discussion
Expect silence to begin with; it’s 
perfectly natural so don’t panic! 
People will be processing what 
they’ve heard and thinking hard so 
allow them a few seconds to do so. 
Be prepared to initiate the 
discussion with one or two simple 
‘starter questions’:
“Where do you see yourself in this 
picture?”
“Which part of the picture speaks to 
you most?”
“What do you think are the barriers/
obstacles in your area?”
“What do we need to be like moving 
forwards?”
Mindset
Being Present
To be fully present and aware for 
the group can be quite a challenge, 
especially if you have a heavy 
workload, have just come out of a 
difficult meeting or have issues that 
are concerning you.
To be of service to the group we 
recommend you spend time prior 
to the session to calm your mind in 
whatever way suits you. 
To alleviate your concerns about 
the session ahead there are some 
simple things you can do, such as 
fully preparing the physical space 
for the session and ensuring you are 
sufficiently familiar with the picture, 
your story and the purpose of the 
session. 
“Which parts of this story do you have 
personal experience of?”
“What is already happening that you 
can get involved in?”
“What needs to happen next?”
“- how would you achieve that?”

24
6: The Facilitation
Self Awareness
It is important to develop a 
conscious awareness of the internal 
conversations or worries that you 
have going on that might distract 
you from you being present for the 
group. It can also be beneficial to be 
aware of certain tendencies you may 
have, such as rescuing or leading a 
conversation, that may impact how 
you are in the session. If you hold 
some strong opinions they may be 
challenged by the views of others 
coming up in the session.
Being Curious and Open Questions
A state of curiosity is the most 
beneficial mind-set to have if you 
are to be of service to the group and 
encourage a discussion between 
them. What you should aim to do is 
to provoke the same curiosity in the 
participants for each other.
The best way of raising this curiosity 
is by ensuring that the questioning 
you use is predominantly Open. This 
is a well-known piece of advice but 
can be surprisingly hard to follow 
when in a session. 
If you find yourself in a session 
where the discussion seems to be 
struggling to get going, then pay 
close attention to the questions 
being asked. The chances are that 
they may be mainly closed.
A closed question is one that 
prompts a straightforward ‘yes’, ‘no’, 
‘here’, ‘that’ or ‘there’ kind of reply. A 
closed question can often be just a 
thinly disguised agenda; a statement 
looking for affirmation. An example 
of this would be a leading question 
such as: “Do you think that this 
new governance is the only way we 
can go forward then?” If you notice 
yourself doing this, try to switch 
from wanting to tell people what 
you think to asking them what they 
think. 

25
Even questions that open with 
“Where…?” and “Who…?” are closed 
because they invite such limited 
answers as “there”, “him”, “her” and 
so on. 
Open questions begin with 
“How…?” “Why…?” and “What….?” 
making it hard for someone not to 
elaborate or give an opinion and so 
are more likely get a conversation 
going. 
Staying out of the discussion
A big challenge in facilitating this 
process is to keep your own views, 
feelings and concerns about the 
subject to yourself during the 
group discussion. It can be quite a 
difficult leap of mindset to go from 
the passionate story sharer to the 
passive facilitator in the space of 
one question. 
The danger is that you can 
unconsciously begin to monopolise 
the discussion and come to be seen 
as the ‘presenter’. The session can 
quickly spiral into a ‘Q&A’ session. 
You may also find yourself on the 
defensive should you encounter 
someone who doesn’t share your 
views or your enthusiasm. 
Making appropriate interventions
Facilitating a session requires you 
to balance between invisibility and 
appropriate interventions. The space 
you leave creates the environment 
for other people to speak and makes 
the focus of the session about their 
opinions, not yours.
However, there are times when you 
may need to make interventions. 
These might be:
• To enable someone who has 
struggled to contribute to 
speak.
• To remind the group the 
purpose of the session e.g. if 
the session develops into a 
‘Q&A’ 
• If people start critiquing the 
picture instead of talking about 
the issues raised by it.
• To challenge someone if they’re 
being disrespectful or offensive
• To challenge an assumption or 
limiting story.
 
Note: if you have been explicit 
about your role at the start your 
interventions will be expected. 

26
6: The Facilitation
Creating dialogue, not Q&A
As a facilitator, your job is to get the
people in your group to engage in 
dialogue with each other, not with 
you. Try to avoid getting locked into 
a ‘Q&A’ mode. If you’re a senior 
member of the team, this is more 
likely than if you’re at the same level 
as the group. If you feel yourself 
being drawn into ‘Q&A’ mode, you 
need to stop it. 
You can do this using some very 
simple techniques. The first one 
is to refrain from answering the 
question yourself. This automatically 
creates a space for someone else to 
share their view. You can combine 
your silence with redirecting the 
question around the group using 
body language and gesture. Imagine 
yourself and your hands as a 
reflective surface and bounce the 
question to someone else.
If you need to, you can share your 
view about the issue at hand, but do 
it with the intention of stimulating 
them to discuss it further. On 
occasion, it can be useful to 
explicitly remind them that your role 
today is more to find out what they 
think than to share what you think.
Key facilitation issues to consider
In our experience there are a 
common concerns people have 
about running a Visual Dialogue 
session. These include:
• What do I do with cynics?
• What if someone dominates the 
group?
• What if people are angry or 
upset?
• What if no-one speaks?
• What if people ask me something 
I don’t know?

27
Our view of the cynic is that they 
weren’t born a cynic. They can be 
very passionate people who are 
frustrated about the situation and 
want change, even if they believe 
it won’t happen. It is important not
to close them down as that has 
probably been their experience in 
the past, but equally you have a 
responsibility to the entire group not 
to let them dominate the discussion. 
A good way of re-framing a cynical 
perspective is to ask them “what 
would you like to have happen?”
Should they be too dominant 
respectfully ask if you could hear 
from someone else in the group. 
Silence can be the single most 
effective tool to create participation 
within the group you’re facilitating. 
If you are uncomfortable with the 
space that silence creates then you 
will be tempted to fill it (you won’t 
be alone!). If you fill it then your 
participants won’t. 
The first place you will notice this 
fear of silence is just after you have 
finished sharing your story and 
have asked your opening question. 
You may notice yourself stretching 
out a question by adding to it or 
re-phrasing. You may follow it up 
with a second question, or a third. If 
you do this the silence will only go 
on longer.
You need to consciously keep the 
question simple, open and short and 
have the courage to let it go.The 
more you talk (for whatever reason) 
the less your group will.
Finally, you are not there to know 
everything about the picture, it is 
complex and you can’t possibly 
know it all.
If someone asks you something you 
don’t know, open it up to the group 
and ask if anyone else does. The 
chances are that someone will.

28
6: The Facilitation
Persecutor
‘I’m OK, 
You’re not OK’
• “He does it deliberately.”
• “You’re wrong.”
• “It’s their fault.”
From a persecutor position a 
person is likely to believe that 
it is the other party that needs 
to change.
• “I’ve tried but it’s no use.”
• “She won’t... so I can’t.”
• “Nothing ever changes.”
From a victim position a person is likely to 
believe that they are powerless to effect any 
change.
Victim
‘I’m not OK’
The Drama Triangle
To facilitate effectively, it is useful 
to develop some awareness of 
group dynamics. If a discussion 
gets difficult, you might see people 
falling into particular patterns or 
roles.
The ‘Drama Triangle’ is a useful tool 
for spotting how people in groups 
often collude together to stay stuck 
in ‘problem thinking’.
The most effective way of reframing a group that may be stuck 
in a problem-thinking mindset is to 
be as curious as possible. “So what 
would you like to have happen?” is 
question that is great for shifting a 
discussion to focus on outcomes.

29
 
any 
• “I’m the one who always has to...”
• “I can’t say no because.”
• “Don’t worry, I’ll do that for you.”
• “Look, I’m only trying to help.” 
From a rescuer position a person is 
likely to:
Attempt to diffuse heated discussions 
Come to the aid of someone who 
may be the focus of a persecutor’s 
attention
Disempower people then intend to 
rescue
Rescuer
‘I’m OK, 
They’re not OK’

30
7. The HT Story - Narrative
1. Current Context
HT is Croatia’s largest telekom 
provider with one of the best 
networks in Europe – it is also 
one of the country’s largest 
employers. This comes with it’s 
challenges though, as it tends 
to be held to high standards of 
expectation. The reputation and 
trust of the telekoms sector in 
Croatia is low, and this highlights 
perceptions of issues that arise for 
HT’s customers such as Reliability, 
Pricing and network reach, as well 
as a suspicion that DT, it’s parent 
company doesn’t have Croatia’s 
best interests in mind. Croatia 
is a nation full of potential and 
Pride but the national mindset
can portray a lack of optimism 
at times, a lack of opportunity or 
personal empowerment, and with 
the pace of how fast things are 
changing in the world there is a 
real danger that Croatia will be left 
behind in Europe.
2. HT Today
This is also reflected within 
the organisation. HT is really 
good at delivering projects on a 
small scale and there is a great 
deal of Trust and Pride within 
team environments – but this 
doesn’t stretch across the whole 
organisation. The Culture is very 
siloed and numbers driven, which 
works well on a small scale but 
doesn’t deliver a common purpose 
for everyone to get behind. 
People are not connected to what 
the “One Goal” is and they feel 
disempowered to do anything 
about this when decision making 
is something done to them. 
However Croatians are tough! 
And they tend to “muscle through” 
when challenges arise. The world 
is changing though and with the 
rise of things like AI a view of 
the future with a clear strategy 
through the fog will be essential.
So what do we do?
3. How We Change
The first thing we must address 
is restoring the Trust from 
our customers, community 
and country.Trust requires – 
Credibility, Reliability, intimacy
and a focus not just on self 
interest.Compassion will be critical 
if we are to understand the needs 
of customers and deal with their 
frustrations. We have a role to 
be Inspiring new possibilities, 
to enable people to Think BIG, 
to embrace and change for the 
future. We are best place to be 
that catalyst for change. We can 
create a cultural shift in Croatia to 

31
one where people feel personal 
power to consider that they can 
do things differently.We need to 
work at these internally as well as 
externally because we all have a 
part to play in delivering this.
4. HT in Future
The greatest opportunity there 
is at the moment is the advent of 
AI. There are many uncertainties 
around AI but there is no doubt 
it will change us all forever and 
how we do things. It will be 
empowering technology that 
will enable people to be more 
productive and innovative in the 
future. We will be essential in 
delivering this, given our scale 
and through partnering with 
industries like Google. We need to 
leading the discussion, especially 
around the ethics of AI, to ensure 
it benefits the whole of Croatia, 
not just the few, and we need to 
build our credibility not just on this 
but across everything we do - To 
be seen that the security of our 
customer’s data is important to 
us, that we deliver fair deals, are 
reliable to deliver, doing what we 
say we will do and that we ensure 
a great customer experience, in 
which they feel in control and 
empowered.We ourselves will 
be AI empowered, enabling us 
to digitise customer journeys 
and ensure that experience is 
the best it can be. But we must 
work together to deliver this, 
communicate better, celebrate 
our successes and be open and 
honest with each other, across the 
organisation.
5. Mission
We are doing a lot of work in the 
community; we are upgrading the 
network to fill the gaps where 
fibre is currently unavailable. 
We are continuously investing in 
Croatia because our aim – our One 
Goal – is to empower everyone 
in Croatia to be able to shape 
their future, to make people 
see opportunities in our country 
and make Croatia the most AI 
empowered society in Europe.

