12. Leadership Principles
Ne gledam probleme 
kroz funkcije - 
sagledavamo ih kroz oči 
korisnika. 
Ne gradim silose - 
funkcioniramo i korisnik 
nas vidi kao jedan tim. 
Ne radim kompromise 
na štetu povjerenja 
- jer povjerenje je 
temelj svake suradnje i 
dugoročnog uspjeha. 
Otvoren/a sam za nove 
pristupe i spreman/a sam 
prilagoditi se kako bih 
ostvario napredak. 
Svjesno koristim svaku 
priliku za učenje i razvoj 
kroz svoje odluke i 
postupke. 
Izazivam sebe i druge da 
izađemo iz zone udonosti 
kako bismo zajedno 
napredovali. 
GRADI POVJERENJE
Zajedno gradimo 
povjerenje kroz 
razumijevanje i empatiju 
prema potrebama 
korisnika.
Otvoreno dijelim 
informacije i podržavam 
suradnju jer vjerujem da 
smo jači zajedno. 
Uvijek biram ono što 
je ispravno, iako nije 
najlakše, kako bih 
očuvao/la povjerenje i 
vjerodostojnost. 
Na meni je. Preuzimam 
osobnu odgovornost. 
Ne postoji tema koja nije 
moja (“netko drugi će”), 
nego pitam: Što ja mogu 
učiniti? 
Ne pristajem na status 
quo - mijenjam se i 
rastem. 
PROMIJENI NA BOLJE

45
Ne zadržavam znanje 
i informacije za sebe 
- inspiriramo druge 
vlastitim primjerom i 
dijelimo znanje kroz 
mentorstvo.
Ne ograničavam se 
na riječi - inspiriram 
kroz konkretne akcije 
i rezultate koji potiču 
druge da naprave isto. 
Ne čekam da promjena 
dođe izvana - pokrećem 
je svojim primjerom i 
pokazujem da se može. 
Ne ignoriram stvarni 
problem - uvijek ga 
adresiramo otvoreno. 
Ne šutim o onome što 
me “boli” - razgovaramo 
o izazovima kroz 
konstruktivnu raspravu. 
Ne izbjegavam teške 
razgovore - jer znam da 
kroz njih rastem(o). 
Pokazujem kako 
djelovanje ima moć 
mijenjati stvari i motiviram 
druge da slijede taj primjer. 
Otvoreno izražavam svoje 
osjećaje i potvrđujem 
tuđe, stvarajući prostor za 
istinsko razumijevanje. 
Prilagođavam svoj pristup 
i ton, vodeći računa o 
emocionalnim potrebama 
svih uključenih. 
Aktivno stvaram 
promjene i time potičem 
tim.
Svjesno pokazujem kako 
dijeljenje znanja može 
podići cijeli tim na višu 
razinu. 
Pritom nastojim osjetiti i 
razumjeti kako se drugi 
osjećaju zbog problema. .
OSLOBODI POTENCIJAL
PODRŽI DRUGE

46
12. Leadership Principles - English
 I do not approach 
challenges through 
functional lenses — we 
focus on understanding 
them from the user’s 
perspective.
I do not build silos — not 
only we operate, but also 
our users perceive us as 
ONE TEAM.
I don’t make 
compromises that 
undermine trust — trust 
is the foundation of 
effective collaboration 
and long-term success.
I am open to new 
approaches and 
demonstrate both the 
readiness and willingness 
to adapt in order to make 
progress.
I consciously use every 
opportunity for learning 
and development through 
my decisions and actions.
I challenge myself and 
others to step outside our 
comfort zones so we can 
advance together.
TRUSTWORTHY
Together, we build trust 
by understanding and 
empathizing with our users’ 
needs.
I openly share information 
and support collaboration 
because I believe we are 
stronger together.
I consistently choose 
what is right, even when 
it’s not the easiest path, 
to preserve trust and 
credibility.
It’s on me. 
I act responsibly.
There is no topic that 
“isn’t mine” or “belongs 
to someone else.” 
Instead, I ask myself: 
“What can I do?”
I do not settle for the 
status quo — I am 
changing and I am 
growing continuously. 
CHANGE-MAKING

47
I openly share 
knowledge and insights 
- we inspire others 
through leading by 
example and mentoring.
I do not rely on words 
alone — I inspire through 
tangible actions and 
results that encourage 
others to do the same.
I am not waiting for 
change to be initiated 
externally — I drive 
it through my own 
behaviour and show that 
progress is possible.
I do not overlook the real 
issue — we address it 
openly.
I do not stay silent about 
what concerns me — 
we discuss challenges 
through constructive 
dialogue.
I do not avoid difficult 
conversations — 
because I know they 
help us grow.
I demonstrate how taking 
action drives real change 
and, in doing so, I inspire 
others to follow that 
example.
I express my own feelings 
openly and acknowledge 
those of others, creating 
space for genuine 
understanding.
I adapt my approach and 
tone with care, keeping in 
mind the emotional needs 
of everyone involved.
I actively drive change 
and energize the team 
through that momentum.
I intentionally 
demonstrate how 
knowledge‑sharing can 
elevate the performance 
and capability of the 
entire team.
At the same time, I make 
an effort to sense and 
understand how others are 
feeling in relation to the 
problem.
INSPIRING
COMPASSIONATE

48
13. Moving to Action - Team Dialogues
Why Action and what do we 
mean?
A great story and a compelling 
“big picture” can inspire people — 
but it only creates change when 
it translates into what we do 
differently day to day.
By “action,” we mean: clear, 
meaningful commitments that 
move our strategy, vision, and 
mission from words into real 
behaviour and results.
A helpful way to frame it is:
• What will we do differently?
• Who will do what?
• By when?
• How will we know it’s 
happening?
Moving from “We” to “I”
Both team commitments and 
individual commitments matter.
Team commitments are powerful 
because they:
• show how we will work 
together
• create shared expectations 
and mutual accountability
• clarify how we will collaborate 
with other teams (“how we do 
‘we’ beyond our team”)
At the same time, team 
commitments can become 
something to hide behind. If we 
only speak in “we,” it can be harder 
to take ownership.
Individual commitments make the 
shift real. They answer:
• “What will I do differently?”
• “What will I stop, start, or 
continue?”
• “How will my team experience 
a change in me?”
The sweet spot: team 
commitments that create 
direction, paired with individual 
actions that build momentum.
What do actions look like?
Good actions are:
• Meaningful: linked to the 
strategy and what your team 
can influence.
• Specific: clear enough that 
someone outside the team 
could understand them
• Behaviour-based: focused on 
what people will do differently
• Measurable: you can notice or 
evidence change
• Owned: there is a named 
person (or clear role) 
accountable
• Realistic: stretching, but 
doable alongside the “day job”

49
A quick test:
If we came back to this in four 
weeks, could we tell whether it 
happened?
Examples of measurable action
Actions don’t need to sound 
“corporate” to be valuable — they 
just need to be observable and 
testable.
Example: Letting go of control
Insight: “I’m going to let go of my 
need for control.”
Meaning: “I recognise my need for 
control comes from wanting things 
to go well — and I also recognise 
that it can be experienced as a 
lack of trust, closing down other 
people’s voices and ideas.”
Action (observable):
• “In meetings, I will ask for two 
other views before I share 
mine.”
• “I will delegate decisions 
under £X / within Y scope to 
the team and support their 
choices.”
How I’ll know it’s changing:
• “I won’t try to solve everything 
myself.”
• “I will hear more ideas coming 
forward, including challenge.”
• “Others will lead parts of 
discussions without looking to 
me for the ‘right answer’.”
You can also encourage teams to 
add a simple measure such as:
• Frequency (“in every weekly 
meeting…”)
• Time (“by the end of the 
month…”)
• Evidence (“we’ll see this in 
our decision log / customer 
feedback / cycle times…”)

50
13. Moving to Action - Team Dialogues
Running Action Planning 
Workshops
Your role in action planning is 
twofold:
1. You are part of the process. 
You are a leader in the team, 
not a neutral observer — your 
willingness to make your own 
commitments sets the tone.
2. You enable the process. 
You guide the group to 
commitments that are 
meaningful, measurable, and 
owned.
What helps most is modelling:
• curiosity over certainty
• openness to challenge
• focus on “what will we do 
differently?”
The Principles
Use these principles to keep action 
planning grounded:
• Action is a choice. We move 
from insight to intentional 
behaviour.
• Action must be meaningful.
It should connect to what 
matters in the strategy, not 
just what feels easy.

51
• Action must be measurable.
If we can’t tell whether it 
happened, it’s probably still an 
intention.
• Action should sit at the 
right level. Not so small 
it’s tokenistic, not so big it 
becomes vague.
Set-up and advice
This is a space where psychological 
safety matters. People may be 
naming behaviours, habits, and 
tensions — so your set-up makes 
the difference.
1. Set-up is key
Be clear on purpose: 
• “This is about making our 
strategy real in how we work.”
• Agree simple working 
agreements (e.g., listen fully, 
assume positive intent, be 
specific, keep it practical).
• Make time for thinking — 
rushing creates generic 
actions.
2. Expect it to take time
Meaningful actions are harder than 
they look. Be kind to yourself and 
your team: the process will take 
as long as it takes. That might feel 
inconvenient-but quality and real 
change matter more than speed.
Things to consider
When you plan the workshop, 
consider where your team is on 
the journey:
• Are they clear on the strategy, 
vision, and mission — or still 
making sense of it?
• Is trust strong enough to name 
real behaviours?
• Do they have space/capacity 
to take on commitments right 
now?
• What will make action feel 
safe, achievable, and relevant? 
• Making space for when we 
slip up

52
13. Moving to Action - Team Dialogues
Supporting questions (draft)
Here are starter questions you can refine:
To move from insight to action
• “What did we hear that matters most for our team?”
• “What needs to change in how we work if we take this strategy 
seriously?”
• “Where are we already aligned — and where are we not?”
To move from ‘we’ to ‘I’
• “What will I do differently in the next two weeks?”
• “What will I stop doing that gets in the way?”
• “What support do I need from the team to follow through?”
To make actions measurable
• “How will we know this is happening?”
• “What will we see/hear more of? Less of?”
• “What’s one small indicator we could track?”

53
To build accountability
• “Who owns this?”
• “When will we check progress?”
• “How will we support each other if we slip back into old habits?”

54
